Mild self-limiting nausea without vomiting following known dietary indiscretion
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Gastric unease without emesis, Dietary indiscretion history, Soft non-tender abdomen

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-023
Reason for Visit: Acute persistent nausea and vague abdominal discomfort requiring physical assessment to rule out early acute appendicitis, cholecystitis, or bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of unremitting nausea and epigastric discomfort. Urgent clinical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to palpate the abdomen, assess for peritoneal signs, and confidently rule out surgical emergencies that present initially with vague gastric unease.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel incredibly sick to my stomach after that huge meal, but I just can't throw up.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a subjective sensation of gastric unease and impending emesis starting 3 hours ago. Describes a direct temporal association with overeating highly rich, fatty foods at a recent event. Notes mild, self-resolving excessive oral salivation. Complains of vague non-focal epigastric discomfort without sharp, radiating pain. Reports a complete absence of actual vomiting episodes. Normal baseline bowel habits without acute diarrhea or constipation.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, currently resting in a semi-recumbent position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Discomfort is causing significant anxiety regarding food poisoning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 86 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, appears mildly uncomfortable and reluctant to move quickly.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, non-distended abdomen on deep clinical palpation. Normal active bowel sounds present in all four quadrants. Diagnostic triad confirmed: gastric unease without emesis, dietary indiscretion history, and a soft non-tender abdomen. No rebound tenderness, guarding, or rigidity. Negative Murphy's sign. Normal vital signs with no resting tachycardia or orthostasis.
Environmental Observations: Leftover takeout containers visible in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient took a single dose of over-the-counter antacid with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild self-limiting nausea without vomiting following known dietary indiscretion - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of focal right upper quadrant pain, negative Murphy's sign, and lack of fever.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Soft abdomen without focal right lower quadrant tenderness, no guarding, and no rebound pain.

Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal active bowel sounds, flat non-distended abdomen, and preserved passage of flatus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of acute, persistent nausea and abdominal discomfort strongly overlaps with the early prodromal phases of serious intra-abdominal surgical emergencies. Evaluating the patient urgently after-hours allowed for a comprehensive abdominal examination. Confirming the absence of peritoneal signs and linking the symptoms to a clear dietary trigger safely avoided unnecessary transfer to the emergency department, ensuring patient stability until regular clinic hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the benign nature of the symptoms. Recommendation for over-the-counter antiemetics (e.g., oral ondansetron wafer) only if symptoms escalate.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink clear fluids in small, frequent sips using a shot glass every 15 minutes, rewarding the stomach with easily absorbed hydration without triggering the stretch receptors that cause reflex vomiting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only if symptoms persist beyond 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop severe, sharp, constant abdominal pain, if your abdomen becomes hard to the touch, or if you begin vomiting blood or material that looks like coffee grounds.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical based on the current benign abdominal exam; early pathology can evolve.
Clinical Course: Nausea is expected to resolve completely over the next 6 to 12 hours as gastric emptying progresses.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if the pain moves to the lower right side of your stomach, or if you become completely unable to keep fluids down.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The stomach was overloaded with rich food, delaying normal emptying and causing stretch and irritation. The nausea is a reflex response. Small sips of water allow the stomach to clear slowly.
Actionable Steps: Rest with your head elevated. Do not force yourself to eat. Take tiny sips of water or clear broth. Avoid fatty or spicy foods for 24 hours.

